# Copy to `.env`. `.env*` is gitignored, so nothing here is ever committed.
#
# Every value below has a working development default *except* DATABASE_URL.
# That asymmetry is deliberate: a missing database fails immediately and
# obviously, whereas a missing secret would otherwise fail subtly, and the
# defaults exist so that a fresh clone runs `next dev` without a scavenger hunt.
# In production, the secrets have no defaults and the process refuses to start.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (required)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The local Postgres runs in Docker. Note the host port is 5433, not 5432 —
# inside the container it is still 5432; only the mapping is moved.
#
#   docker run -d --name ncert-pg \
#     -e POSTGRES_USER=ncert -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=ncert -e POSTGRES_DB=ncert \
#     -p 5433:5432 postgres:16
#
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://ncert:ncert@localhost:5433/ncert?schema=public"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the session cookie. At least 16 characters. Rotating it signs everyone
# out at once, which is the only revocation lever there is — see the note at the
# top of src/lib/session.ts.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
SESSION_SECRET=""

# Signs OTP challenge tokens and local storage URLs. Both fall back to
# SESSION_SECRET when unset, which is fine for development and worth separating
# in production so that rotating one does not invalidate the other.
OTP_SECRET=""
STORAGE_URL_SECRET=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# "local" (default) writes to .storage/ and serves through /api/dev/storage/.
# "s3" is the production shape and is not implemented yet — it throws rather
# than silently writing nowhere.
STORAGE_DRIVER="local"
# Where the local driver writes. Defaults to ./.storage. Must be gitignored.
STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign-in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is nothing to configure here, which is the point of writing it down.
#
# Two ways in, both server-side only:
#
#   POST /api/auth/register/  + /api/auth/login/   email and a password
#   POST /api/auth/otp/request/ + /api/auth/otp/verify/   a code to a phone
#
# Passwords are scrypt (node:crypto, no dependency), salted per user, and the
# parameters are stored in the hash so they can be raised later. Both paths mint
# the same session cookie, signed with SESSION_SECRET above.
#
# The role a new account gets is STUDENT, always, in production. Outside
# production the sign-in screen offers a role picker so one person can hold a
# student, evaluator, parent and admin account; there is no flag that turns
# that on in production, only NODE_ENV that turns it off.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no SMS provider, and this repo does not add one. In development the
# OTP is deterministic (derived from the phone number) and printed to the server
# log; `GET /api/dev/login/?phone=+91...` returns it directly. In production,
# `deliverOtp()` in src/lib/auth.ts throws until a provider is wired in — a
# sign-in that returns 200 and sends nothing is worse than one that fails.
#
# The OTP flow is still there and still works; email and password exist because
# it cannot let anyone into a production deployment until a provider does.
